DMbot - TESTING STATUS (current testing only)
=============================================
Last updated: 2026-10-04 by the PyCharm session

What this file is
  The immediate live-testing picture: what's next, what's blocking it, and the latest
  result. It is kept SHORT: finished items move to docs/testing-history.log (the full
  record, append-only), and this file is trimmed. The PyCharm session maintains both.
  The web session reads them; it doesn't need copied terminal output.
  Never put tokens, .env contents or players' personal data here (the repo is public).

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT TEST: Test 1, run 2: voice capture with 2 people (docs/LIVE_TEST.md)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Why
  Run 1 (2026-10-04) had only the DM. It was a partial pass: consent revoke, two
  speakers at once, and overlapping speech weren't tested. The audio % was
  borderline, but the cause has since been fixed (#36, PR #46).

Before the test (owner)
  [ ] .env: add DMBOT_DEBUG_AUDIO=1
  [ ] A second person in the voice channel (a phone with a second account is fine)
  [ ] Optional: the "clumsy" tool (Windows) to add ~5% packet loss for the sensitivity check

Before the test (PyCharm session)
  [ ] git fetch, check out development, pull; pip install -e ".[dev]" in core; npm ci in ears
  [ ] Start core, then ears; confirm "ears connected"

Commands to use
  /table join, /consent give, /consent revoke, /table status, /table leave
  (/dmbot start is planned in #48 and NOT built yet)

What to check (in addition to LIVE_TEST.md step 5)
  - Debug line per utterance in the ears terminal:
      [ears] audio user=... received=... expected=... pauses=... paused_ms=... link_dropped_total=...
  - Long sentences with short pauses should read 95-100% with pauses > 0
  - With ~5% loss injected, the % should DROP. That's the proof the #36 fix doesn't hide loss.
  - link_dropped_total stays at 0
  - The second person runs /consent revoke and disappears from the next capture checks
  - The ⚠️ flag, not the rounded %, decides pass or fail (#44)

